Last-Update: 2026-11-07
From 806b1ebb3bb42a4bd2eb9e91aa667c2b1c8c97c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alia Holes <thealiaholes@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 21:08:20 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] sms: shrink images to fit before sending, off the main
 thread

Picture attachments were base64-encoded as they are, and the SMS app
refused anything over 600 kB ("Max message size limit exceeded"), so a
normal phone photo could not be sent at all.

Re-encode every still image (not GIFs) before sending: apply the EXIF
orientation, convert to sRGB, flatten onto white, and write a JPEG that fits
a per-message image budget (default 300 kB, shared by all images of the
message), lowering the quality first and then the resolution. The output
carries no metadata, so location and camera data no longer leave with the
picture. Other files are sent unchanged and still have to fit the message
limit (default 600 kB).

Reading, scaling and encoding run in QtConcurrent, so the daemon keeps
serving D-Bus while a big photo is prepared. A file that cannot be read or
decoded, or a message that still does not fit, is no longer sent half-empty:
the daemon emits messageSendFailed with a reason instead (and a send that
cannot be handed to the link counts toward "not syncing"). The SMS app's
size check counts images at their budget rather than their file size.

sendSms may be called from any thread (a worker that first fetched the
conversation from the phone has no event loop of ours): it re-queues itself
onto the plugin's thread before it starts the attachment work.

Tunable per device: mmsImageBudgetBytes and mmsMaxMessageBytes.
